      "id": "one-kind-thought",
      "title": "Offer one kind thought",
      "minutes": 2,
      "theme_label": "compassion",
      "body": "Compassion does not need a grand gesture. One kind thought — toward yourself or someone else — can shift the weather of a whole hour.\n\nKindness often gets postponed until you feel you have earned it. Offer it first. A quieter inner sentence (“This is hard, and I’m still here”) costs little and changes how you move.\n\nIf someone frustrates you, try one charitable thought before the critique: maybe they are tired, overloaded, or missing context. You can still hold a boundary; you do not need contempt as fuel.\n\nFor yourself, replace one harsh label with a plainer description. “I made a messy choice” lands differently than “I always ruin things.” Accuracy is kinder than exaggeration.\n\nScatter one deliberate kind thought today. Let it be specific and brief. Compassion practised in small doses becomes a habit your nervous system recognises.\n\nScatter kindness like seeds, not spotlights. One sincere thought is enough practice for today; repetition over weeks is what changes the inner climate.",
      "practice": "Offer one specific kind thought (to yourself or someone else) and hold it for ten seconds. No need to announce it — just mean it.",
